  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline>
   {"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://ryansrolfing.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Specialties"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Sciatica"}]}
  </script>

  <article class="px-5 py-20 md:py-28">
   <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
    <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.28em] text-[#c4924a]">
     Sciatica and leg-pain patterns
    </p>
    <h1 class="mt-5 text-5xl leading-tight md:text-7xl">
     Rolfing for Sciatica in Folsom
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9 text-[#545454]/85">
     Sciatica is a name people use for pain, tingling, or aching that travels from the low back or buttock into the leg. Rolfing does not treat the nerve. It looks at the pattern around it: pelvis, hips, low back, legs, and feet.
    </p>
   </div>
   <div class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-3xl space-y-12 text-lg leading-9 text-[#545454]/85">
    <section>
     <h2 class="text-4xl leading-tight text-[#545454]">
      The symptom and the pattern
     </h2>
     <p class="mt-5">
      The same word covers different problems: a disc irritating a nerve, a tired hip, a pelvic tilt, or a long habit of standing on one leg. Those are not the same thing. Ryan does not diagnose which one you have.
     </p>
     <p class="mt-5">
      What a session can do is look at how the pelvis, hips, ribs, and legs are organizing, and whether the low back and hip are being asked to brace all day.
     </p>
    </section>
    <section>
     <h2 class="text-4xl leading-tight text-[#545454]">
      What a session may include
     </h2>
     <p class="mt-5">
      Work may include the hips, gluteal area, hamstrings, calves, feet, low back, and ribs — wherever one side is doing too much. Walking matters here, because this kind of pain often shows up in how you step, sit, and get out of a car.
     </p>
     <p class="mt-5">
      Pressure stays collaborative. The aim is not to dig at a nerve or chase the pain down the leg.
     </p>
    </section>
    <section>
     <h2 class="text-4xl leading-tight text-[#545454]">
      Clear limits
     </h2>
     <p class="mt-5">
      Rolfing is not medical care for sciatica, a disc herniation, or nerve injury. It will not “release a pinched nerve” on demand, and it is not a substitute for imaging or an exam when symptoms are serious.
     </p>
     <p class="mt-5">
      See a doctor promptly for leg pain with progressive weakness, numbness in the groin, bowel or bladder changes, fever, or pain after a significant injury.
     </p>
    </section>
   </div>
   <section class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-4xl rounded-[2rem] bg-[#545454] p-8 text-white md:p-10">
    <h2 class="text-4xl text-white">
     This work may fit if you notice
    </h2>
    <ul class="mt-7 list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-white/82">
     <li>
      Buttock or leg pain that eases, then returns with the same activities.
     </li>
     <li>
      A one-sided pattern tied to sitting, driving, or standing.
     </li>
     <li>
      Low back and hip tension that travel together.
     </li>
     <li>
      You have already been checked medically and want structural work alongside that care.
     </li>
    </ul>
   </section>
   <section class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-3xl">
    <h2 class="text-4xl leading-tight">
     Questions people ask
    </h2>
    <div class="mt-8 space-y-4">
     <details class="rounded-2xl bg-[#f8f6f2] p-6">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer text-2xl text-[#545454]">
       Can Rolfing help sciatica?
      </summary>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-8 text-[#545454]/85">
       Rolfing may support some people whose buttock or leg pain is tied to posture, hip and pelvic strain, and how the low back shares load. It does not diagnose or treat nerve injury, and it is not a cure for sciatica.
      </p>
     </details>
     <details class="rounded-2xl bg-[#f8f6f2] p-6">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer text-2xl text-[#545454]">
       Do you work on the nerve?
      </summary>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-8 text-[#545454]/85">
       No. Sessions work with fascia, the soft tissue around the pelvis and legs, and movement patterns. Ryan does not claim to free a trapped nerve.
      </p>
     </details>
     <details class="rounded-2xl bg-[#f8f6f2] p-6">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer text-2xl text-[#545454]">
       Should I come in during a bad flare?
      </summary>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-8 text-[#545454]/85">
       If you cannot walk, the pain is severe, or weakness or numbness is new, get medical care first. If the symptoms are familiar and you can move enough to lie down and walk a little, a session can be paced to what you can tolerate that day.
      </p>
     </details>
     <details class="rounded-2xl bg-[#f8f6f2] p-6">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer text-2xl text-[#545454]">
       When should I see a doctor first?
      </summary>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-8 text-[#545454]/85">
       Seek medical care first for sudden or severe leg pain, progressive weakness, saddle numbness, bowel or bladder changes, fever, unexplained weight loss, or pain after trauma.
      </p>
     </details>
    </div>
   </section>
   <section class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-3xl space-y-6 text-lg leading-9 text-[#545454]/85">
    <h2 class="text-4xl leading-tight text-[#545454]">
     Sciatica bodywork in Folsom
    </h2>
    <p>
     Ryan’s Rolfing is located at 101 Parkshore Drive, Folsom, CA 95630, and serves clients from Folsom, El Dorado Hills, Shingle Springs, Cameron Park, Placerville, and the greater Sacramento area.
    </p>
